sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch10-03> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Randomness cannot usefully be defined only by the existence of short descriptions" ;
    dct:description "Defining something as random only when no short description of it exists at all is too restrictive and uncomputable (it would mean deterministic rules can never generate randomness), so any useful definition of randomness must refer to how short descriptions are to be found." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 10, “Processes of Perception and Analysis”; section “Defining the Notion of Randomness” (from p. 552), p. 555. Scope: definitions of randomness, including description-length (algorithmic) definitions" ;
    dct:type "conceptual" .
}

